  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This bioRxiv preprint is still in the early stages of scholarly uptake, but the record already shows one citation and two documented downstream reuse signals. Its influence is concentrated within related virology and interferon-response research, suggesting that the work is beginning to inform follow-on studies on Zika virus NS5 function.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest available signal is the article\u2019s early scholarly uptake: OpenAlex records one citation, and the reuse graph shows two downstream reuses. Taken together, these indicators suggest that the study is already being used as a reference point in adjacent work, even though the evidence base remains modest at this stage.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article\u2019s scientific focus is mechanistically oriented and timely for stem research in flavivirus biology. By examining how Zika virus nonstructural protein 5 balances RNA replication with interferon antagonism, the authors address a core evolutionary constraint in viral immune evasion. The concept profile reinforces this framing, with emphasis on virology, flavivirus biology, STAT2, interferon, viral replication, and viral evolution.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape shows that the work is being read within a related preprint ecosystem rather than across a broad set of venues. The single citing work is itself a bioRxiv preprint, and the surrounding concepts point to interferon and immune-response research as well as mosquito-borne disease control. That pattern is consistent with an emerging, topic-specific conversation around NS5-mediated immune evasion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is no documented public-discourse signal in the available record, and the paper\u2019s broader reach is therefore best characterized as scholarly rather than public-facing. The article is openly accessible, and repository records indicate multiple open instances, which may help support reuse and visibility as the work moves from preprint circulation toward further citation and validation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, this is an early-impact paper with a focused but credible footprint: one citation, two documented reuses, and a clear fit within ongoing research on flavivirus immune antagonism. The record suggests that the study is already serving as a useful molecular and evolutionary reference, while the broader impact trajectory will depend on how the findings are taken up in subsequent experimental and translational work.</p>",
